Extracting Context-helped pair
By context-helped pair here, I mean by finding the question that model got it wrong without context and being able to get it correct with the retrieved contexts. 
Then, we first save the mean diff or the vector(correct - incorrect) in model’s activation space at the corresponding layers we later do SAE on. 
Then, hook the SAE layers, and put the activation into SAE.
Here, we are trying to find, for a specific task family (questions from a similar setup that need similar subroutine so solve), the candidate features that are fired up intensively across the questions from that task family that model solve it correctly with the context but the features did not get fired when the model fail initially without context. 
Feature discovery: For each generation, we extract per-token SAE activations across all generated tokens (not prompt tokens). We record per-token top-k sparse activations (indices and values) and compute three summary statistics per feature: sum activation, max activation, and top-k count across the generation. For candidate screening, we use the mean activation vector across generated tokens to compute effect sizes between positive/negative pairs. We validate candidates with token-level activation traces showing where in the generation each feature fires.
Check: do these candidate features also fire when the model succeeds without context on other problems?
Intervention: In mech interpre, need bidirectional to make a strong claim and identify causal rather correlation 
Ablation
With earlier candidate features identified, we will first do ablation. The exact way we do it is we first take the activation, we project into the SAE sparse high D space, we zero out those candidate features, and then, we decode it back to model dimension and let model continue inference from there.  
